+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of seconds,
+like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit:
+
+- `d` for days,
+- `h` for hours,
+- `m` for minutes,
+- `s` for seconds.
+
+Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, without spaces between
+groups. Units are case-insensitive, so `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S`, and `1d2h3m4s`
+are all accepted. Anything else, such as negative or decimal numbers, unknown,
+repeated or out-of-order units, or spaces, is rejected. A `timedelta` default
+is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
